---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { DOC_ITEMS } from "../../data/docs";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="overview"
  title="Overview"
  description="Corvene is a native recreation of GitHub Desktop written in Rust: the same interface and workflow, a different engine."
>
  <div class="note note-success">
    <svg class="text-success" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M0 8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.5h-2.5a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z" />
    </svg>
    <p>
      <strong>Status: pre-release.</strong> Feature-complete with GitHub Desktop 3.6.6 on macOS, minus a few gaps. Linux
      runs the same app with GitHub Desktop's Linux menus and wording and is not yet pixel-identical everywhere. Windows
      builds are new. Android is experimental.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why">Why Corvene</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub Desktop has the best-understood Git workflow of any desktop client: a clear changes list, effortless branching,
    pull requests a click away, and years of muscle memory in many hands. It is also an Electron application. It idles at
    hundreds of megabytes, starts slowly and renders through a browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    Corvene keeps the interface and replaces the engine. Every layout constant, menu, dialog and shortcut follows
    <a href="https://github.com/desktop/desktop/releases/tag/release-3.6.6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Desktop 3.6.6</a>
    (the <a href={`${base}/docs/parity`}>parity harness</a> checks that). Underneath there is no Chromium and no Node:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>GPUI</strong>, the GPU-accelerated UI framework behind the Zed editor, draws the window through Metal on
      macOS and Vulkan on Linux and Android.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>gitoxide</strong> reads the repository in process: status, history, diffs and file contents without
      spawning a process for each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own git</strong> performs every write (commit, checkout, merge, rebase, push, stash), so hooks,
      credential helpers and your configuration behave exactly as they do in GitHub Desktop.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No telemetry.</strong> Nothing is reported anywhere.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="improvements">Improvements, each behind a flag</h2>
  <p>
    Where Corvene does something GitHub Desktop does not, from a filesystem watcher to commit templates and optional
    tree-sitter highlighting for 300+ languages, the change is a <a href={`${base}/docs/flags`}>flag</a>. The
    <em>GitHub Desktop</em> preset switches every one of them off, bugs included, so the two apps can be compared side by
    side or used interchangeably.
  </p>

  <h2 id="platforms">Platforms</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Platform</th><th>State</th><th>Packages</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>macOS 10.15.7+ (Intel), 11+ (Apple Silicon)</td><td>Complete, pixel-checked</td><td>Homebrew cask, .dmg, .zip</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linux (glibc 2.35+, X11 or Wayland)</td><td>Complete, small visual differences</td><td>.deb, .rpm, AppImage, Flatpak, snap, .tar.gz, AUR</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Windows 10 and 11</td><td>New, less tested</td><td>Installer, .msi, portable zip</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Android 8.0+</td><td>Experimental</td><td>APK (git, OpenSSH and Git LFS bundled)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    {DOC_ITEMS.filter((d) => d.slug !== "overview").map((d) => (
      <li>
        <a href={`${base}/docs/${d.slug}`}>{d.title}</a>: {d.description}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
